-- title:
--   Lemma EC.8 — the critical prices $P_1,P_2,P_3$ of Definition EC.1 exist, satisfy (EC.8.4)–(EC.8.5), and lie below $P^{NV}$
-- statement:
--   Throughout, the standing model of §3.1 holds (the published `HedgePSNV.Model.Standing`): $B,\tilde B$ independent Brownian motions with augmented filtrations $\mathcal F\supseteq\mathcal G$, the asset $dX_t=X_t(\mu_t\,dt+\sigma_t\,dB_t)$, the demand component $C$ with $A_T=C_T+\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$, the two Markov hypotheses, and $\mathbb P^M$ with $d\mathbb P^M/d\mathbb P=Z_T$ (12); $V_0(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)]$, and $D=\{(P,R):P\ge c,\ R-bP\ge0\}$.
--
--   Suppose Assumptions 1–5 hold (Assumption 1 under $\mathbb P$ and $\mathbb P^M$; Assumptions 2–3 at a maximizer $(P^{NV},R^{NV})$ of problem (2)) and $C_T\preceq C^M_T$; let $m=\mathbb E[H_T(P^{NV},R^{NV})]$ (the setting of §EC.8.3.1). With the section maximizers $P^{NV(M)}(\cdot)$, $R^{NV(M)}(\cdot)$ of $V_0$ over the sections of $D$, Definition EC.1 sets: $P_1$ the smallest solution of $V_0(P_1,R^{NV(M)}(P_1))=m$, $R_1=R^{NV(M)}(P_1)$; $P_2$ the solution of $R^{NV(M)}(P_2)=R^{NV}$; $P_3$ the smallest solution of $V_0(P_3,R^{NV})=m$. Then:
--   1. $P_1$ exists and $P_1\le P^{NV}$;
--   2. $P_2$ exists, is unique, and
--   $$
--   P_2=\frac{c-s\,\mathbb P^M(A_T\le R^{NV})}{1-\mathbb P^M(A_T\le R^{NV})}=s+\frac{c-s}{\mathbb P^M(A_T\ge R^{NV})}\le P^{NV}\qquad(\text{EC.8.4});
--   $$
--   3. $P_3$ exists, $P_3\le P^{NV}$, and for $P^{NV(M)}(R^{NV})$ any section maximizer of $V_0(\cdot,R^{NV})$,
--   $$
--   P_3=P^{NV(M)}(R^{NV})-\sqrt{\big(P^{NV(M)}(R^{NV})-P^{NV}\big)\big(P^{NV(M)}(R^{NV})+P^{NV}-2s\big)}\qquad(\text{EC.8.5}).
--   $$
--
--   These critical values reduce Theorem 3(ii)–(iii) to comparisons of $P_1,P_2,P_3$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $P_1$ is the least element of the set of $P$ at which some section maximizer $R$ of $V_0(P,\cdot)$ has $V_0(P,R)=m$; $P_2$ is stated as: the set of $P$ at which $R^{NV}$ is a section maximizer of $V_0(P,\cdot)$ is the singleton given by (EC.8.4); $P_3$ is the least real root of the quadratic $V_0(\cdot,R^{NV})=m$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: $\mathcal F_t$ and $\mathcal G_t$ are the filtrations generated by $(B_t,\tilde B_t)$ and $B_t$ (p.11) → the $\mathbb P$-augmented natural filtrations of $(B,\tilde B)$ and of $B$ (published `HedgePSNV.Model.Standing`); reason: martingale representation (Prop. EC.3) holds for the augmented Brownian filtration, and null-set modifications of an adapted process must stay adapted
--   - reading: $(X_t,\mu_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P$ and $(X_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P^M$ (pp.11–12) → the simple Markov property on $[0,T]$ with respect to $\mathcal F$ (`IsMarkovWrt`); reason: the paper does not define the term; this is the standard definition, relative to the filtration of footnote 4
--   - encoding: processes on $[0,T]$; stochastic integrals with almost-sure integrability → processes indexed by $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$, constrained for $t\le T$; the published relation `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral` plus a pathwise drift integral; reason: values after $T$ never enter; under the augmented filtration the integrand classes agree up to indistinguishability (published base convention)
--   - reading: $c$ the unit production cost, $s$ the unit salvage value, $X_0$ an asset price → $s<c$ and $x_0>0$ in the published parameter structure; reason: the newsvendor convention (p.7), implied by Assumption 3 wherever it is assumed; $X_t>0$ is used on p.ec28
--   - reading: $(P^{NV},R^{NV})$ the solution of problem (2), named by Assumptions 2–3 → any maximizer of $\mathbb E[H_T]$ over $D$ at which Assumptions 2 and 3 hold; reason: uniqueness is the content of Proposition 1 and is not built into a definition
--   - reading: Assumption 1 on the distribution of the market size $A_T$ → Assumption 1 for the law of $A_T$ under $\mathbb P$ and for its law under $\mathbb P^M$; reason: the paper applies the newsvendor analysis under both measures (Table EC.3; proofs on pp.ec49–ec57 use $F_M$ and $f_M$), as in the published base items
--   - encoding: $C_T\preceq C^M_T$, with $C^M_T$ the $\mathbb P$-version of the $\mathbb P^M$-law of $C_T$ and $X\preceq Y$ iff $\mathbb P(Y\le a)\le\mathbb P(X\le a)$ (p.12) → `UsualOrder P P^M C_T C_T`: $\mathbb P(C_T>x)\le\mathbb P^M(C_T>x)$ for all $x$; reason: the law of $C^M_T$ under $\mathbb P$ is the law of $C_T$ under $\mathbb P^M$; for probability measures the tail form is the distribution-function form
--   - reading: $P^{NV(M)}(R):=\arg\max_PV_0(P,R)$, $R^{NV(M)}(P):=\arg\max_RV_0(P,R)$ (EC.8.2), (EC.8.9) → every maximizer over the section of $D$ (`IsSecMaxP`, `IsSecMaxR`); reason: the convention of the published Lemma EC.7: with the unconstrained formula $R^{NV(M)}(c)=F_M^{-1}(0)=-\infty$; the closed forms are the section maximizers wherever they lie in the section
--   - reading: Lemma EC.8 prints no hypotheses (it sits in §EC.8.3.1, “Throughout the proof, m denotes … E[H_T(P^NV,R^NV)] > 0”) → the hypotheses of Theorem 3 that do not involve $B$: Assumptions 1–5 and $C_T\preceq C^M_T$, with $m=\mathbb E[H_T(P^{NV},R^{NV})]$; reason: the section states it inside the proof of Theorem 3 and its proof uses $A_T\preceq A^M_T$ and Proposition 1
--   - reading: “All $P_1$, $P_2$ and $P_3$ exist, hence by definition they are unique” → existence of the least solutions $P_1,P_3$ and existence and uniqueness of the solution $P_2$ (singleton set); reason: $P_1$ and $P_3$ are defined as smallest solutions, unique by definition; $P_2$ is defined as “the solution”
--   - encoding: $V_0(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)]$, presupposed finite → no extra hypothesis; reason: under $C_T\preceq C^M_T$ the negative part of $A_T$ is smaller under $\mathbb P^M$ than under $\mathbb P$, where it is integrable because $\mathbb E[H_T(P^{NV},R^{NV})]>0$ (Assumption 2); so $V_0$ is the paper's value
-- source:
--   Wang–Yao–Zhang, How Does Risk Hedging Impact Operations? Insights from a Price-Setting Newsvendor Model, arXiv:2201.01026v4, Lemma EC.8 and Definition EC.1, pp.ec50–ec51 (PDF 86–87)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HedgePSNV_Supplement_SectionMax
import Definitions.Def_StochasticOrders_Usual_UsualOrder

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace HedgePSNV.Supplement

open HedgePSNV

/-- Lemma EC.8, arXiv:2201.01026v4, p.ec51, in the setting of §EC.8.3.1 (p.ec50: the proof of
Theorem 3; Assumptions 1–5, `C_T ⪯ C^M_T`, and `m = E[H_T(P^NV, R^NV)] > 0`). With the
critical values of Definition EC.1 (p.ec50) —
(i) `P1` the smallest solution of `E^M[H_T(P1, R^NV(M)(P1))] = m` (and `R1 := R^NV(M)(P1)`),
(ii) `P2` the solution of `R^NV(M)(P2) = R^NV`, (iii) `P3` the smallest solution of
`V_0(P3, R^NV) = m` — all `P1`, `P2` and `P3` exist, hence by definition they are unique;
`P2 = (c − sP^M(A_T ≤ R^NV))/(1 − P^M(A_T ≤ R^NV)) = s + (c − s)/P^M(A_T ≥ R^NV)` (EC.8.4);
`P3 = P^NV(M)(R^NV) − √((P^NV(M)(R^NV) − P^NV)(P^NV(M)(R^NV) + P^NV − 2s))` (EC.8.5); and
`Pᵢ ≤ P^NV`, `i = 1, 2, 3` (EC.8.6).

Precisely: `P1` is the least element of the set of prices `P` at which some section maximizer
`R` of `V_0(P, ·)` has `V_0(P, R) = m`; the set of `P` at which `R^NV` is a section maximizer of
`V_0(P, ·)` is the singleton `{P2}` with `P2` given by (EC.8.4); `P3` is the least real root of
`V_0(·, R^NV) = m`, and (EC.8.5) holds for every section maximizer `P^NV(M)(R^NV)` of
`V_0(·, R^NV)`.

**Formalization Note** Section maximizers are taken over the sections of the decision domain
(`IsSecMaxP`, `IsSecMaxR`), the convention of the published Lemma EC.7. At a solution of (i)
the unconstrained maximizer `F_M^{-1}((P1 − c)/(P1 − s))` is at least `bP1` (otherwise
`V_0 < 0 < m`), so the two readings define the same `P1` and `P2`. `C_T ⪯ C^M_T` is
`UsualOrder P P^M C_T C_T`; Assumption 1 is imposed on the laws of `A_T` under `P` and `P^M`. -/
theorem lemma_ec_8 (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : Model p Ω)
    (hM : M.Standing) (h4 : M.Assumption4) (h5 : M.Assumption5)
    (h1P : Assumption1 (M.P.map M.AT)) (h1M : Assumption1 (M.PM.map M.AT))
    (PNV RNV : ℝ) (hNV : IsNVMaximizer p M.P M.AT PNV RNV)
    (h2 : Assumption2 p M.P M.AT PNV RNV) (h3 : Assumption3 p M.P M.AT PNV RNV)
    (hneg : StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder M.P M.PM (M.C p.T) (M.C p.T)) :
    (∃ P1 : ℝ, IsLeast {P : ℝ | ∃ R : ℝ, IsSecMaxR M P R ∧
        M.V0 P R = nvValue p M.P M.AT PNV RNV} P1 ∧ P1 ≤ PNV) ∧
    {P : ℝ | IsSecMaxR M P RNV} =
      {(p.c - p.s * (M.PM {ω | M.AT ω ≤ RNV}).toReal) / (1 - (M.PM {ω | M.AT ω ≤ RNV}).toReal)} ∧
    (p.c - p.s * (M.PM {ω | M.AT ω ≤ RNV}).toReal) / (1 - (M.PM {ω | M.AT ω ≤ RNV}).toReal) =
      p.s + (p.c - p.s) / (M.PM {ω | RNV ≤ M.AT ω}).toReal ∧
    (p.c - p.s * (M.PM {ω | M.AT ω ≤ RNV}).toReal) / (1 - (M.PM {ω | M.AT ω ≤ RNV}).toReal) ≤
      PNV ∧
    (∃ P3 : ℝ, IsLeast {P : ℝ | M.V0 P RNV = nvValue p M.P M.AT PNV RNV} P3 ∧ P3 ≤ PNV ∧
      ∀ Po : ℝ, IsSecMaxP M Po RNV →
        P3 = Po - Real.sqrt ((Po - PNV) * (Po + PNV - 2 * p.s))) := by sorry

end HedgePSNV.Supplement
